.TH QUICKTIME 2
.SH NAME
quicktime \- QuickTime and MP4 file structure
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "quicktime.m";
qt := load QuickTime QuickTime->PATH;

Sample: adt {
	off:	big;
	size:	int;
	delta:	int;
	sync:	int;
};

Track: adt {
	id:		int;
	kind:		string;
	codec:		string;
	timescale:	int;
	width:		int;
	height:		int;
	extra:		array of byte;
	samples:	array of Sample;
};

init:	fn();
tracks:	fn(file: string): (array of ref Track, string);
open:	fn(file: string): (ref QD, string);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tracks
parses a QuickTime or MP4 file and returns one
.B Track
for each media track, with everything needed to hand its samples to a
decoder.
It walks the file by seeking, because the sample tables live in
.B moov
while the samples they describe live in
.BR mdat ,
and the two appear in either order.
.PP
.B Kind
is the handler type,
.B vide
or
.B soun
and so on;
.B codec
is the sample format, such as
.BR avc1 .
.B Timescale
is the number of time units per second, in which each sample's
.B delta
is expressed.
.B Width
and
.B height
are meaningful for video tracks.
.PP
.B Extra
is the codec setup data, verbatim: for
.B avc1
it is the
.B avcC
payload carrying the sequence and picture parameter sets.
A hardware decoder must be configured with this before it can be given
any sample, so a track whose
.B extra
is nil cannot be decoded even though its samples are present.
.PP
.B Coff
is the composition offset: a sample's presentation time is its decode
time plus this.
It is non-zero only when the stream codes frames out of display order,
which H.264 does whenever it uses B-frames, and then samples arrive in
decode order and a player must reorder them.
Ignoring it produces pictures in the wrong order \(em which looks like
a broken decoder and is not one.
.PP
Each
.B Sample
gives a file offset and length, so the coded bytes can be read without
further parsing, its duration in the track's timescale, and whether it
is a sync sample \(em one that can be decoded without reference to
anything before it.
Where the file has no sync sample table, every sample is reported as a
sync sample, which is what an all-keyframe codec produces.
.PP
.B Open
and the
.B QD
reader are older and much more limited: they parse the movie and track
.I headers
sequentially and know nothing of sample tables.
New code wants
.BR tracks .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/quicktime.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR qttest (1),
.IR draw (3)
.SH BUGS
Composition offsets are read as signed, including in a version 0
.B ctts
where the specification says unsigned, because real files \(em
including this tree's own test movie, written by the platform's encoder
\(em carry negative values there.
.PP
.B Tracks
reads each sample table whole, so a movie with a very large number of
samples costs memory proportional to that count \(em not to the size of
the movie, but not nothing either.
.PP
Only the setup box is extracted from a sample description, and only
.BR avcC ,
.B hvcC
and
.B esds
are recognised.
Multiple sample descriptions in one track are ignored: the first is
used for the whole track.
.PP
Edit lists are not applied, so the samples are in decode order with
their raw durations, which is not necessarily presentation order or
timing.
